{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the spec from user turns only, and keep it cumulative\nTreat the task spec as a running ledger of **explicit user-stated constraints**. Merge each new user turn into the current spec immediately, because later shards often add the key filter, output shape, or scope limit. Do not pull in assistant guesses, domain conventions, or inferred intent as if they were user requirements.\n\n### 2) Re-check earlier assistant behavior after each new user shard\nJudge the assistant against the **full accumulated user intent**, not the partial view available at the time of an earlier turn. A response that looked acceptable before a later shard may become stale or wrong once the user adds a missing condition. Multi-turn analysis should catch drift over the trajectory, not only the final message.\n\n### 3) Treat omissions as first-class failures, not secondary issues\nA common divergence is not an explicit contradiction but a **missing user-stated constraint**. If the assistant answers the right domain but leaves out a required field, filter, conjunct, or granularity, flag that as a real error. Missing one required condition is enough to make an otherwise plausible answer incomplete.\n\n### 4) Separate \u201cright topic\u201d from \u201cright completion\u201d\nDo not collapse surface relevance into correctness. An assistant can be on the correct subject, use the right vocabulary, or make partial progress while still failing the actual task because it missed a binding requirement. Preserve partial alignment, but mark it insufficient when the user\u2019s full objective is not satisfied.\n\n### 5) Watch for premature narrowing and unsupported commitment\nIf the user has not fully specified the task yet, any assistant move that commits to a narrow interpretation, selection rule, output shape, or decomposition is suspicious unless directly supported by the user text. The key diagnostic is whether the assistant assumed a narrower task than the user actually gave, especially before later shards arrived.\n\n### 6) Use the latest user specification as the authority\nWhen earlier turns are ambiguous, the most recent user message that adds or refines constraints should control the analysis. Earlier assumptions should be re-evaluated in light of later disclosure. This prevents the analyzer from overvaluing the first interpretation simply because it appeared first.\n\n### 7) Diagnose in constraint terms, not style terms\nPrefer labels like **missing constraint**, **extra assumption**, **scope drift**, **narrowed task**, **unsupported commitment**, or **shape mismatch**. These make the mismatch actionable. Broad judgments like \u201cincomplete\u201d or \u201cnot good\u201d are less useful than naming exactly which user requirement was missed, altered, or added.\n\n### 8) Treat output shape as a requirement, not a formatting detail\nExact fields, aggregation level, ordering, filtering criteria, and requested granularity are substantive constraints. An answer can mention the correct entities and still diverge if it returns the wrong shape or level of detail. Check whether the assistant respected the requested form, not just the topic.\n\n### 9) Prefer the earliest mismatch when explaining what changed\nWhen multiple issues exist, identify the **first point of divergence** from user requirements. Early wrong assumptions often contaminate later reasoning and outputs. Naming the earliest constraint mismatch helps the corrective step remove the anchor error rather than patching downstream symptoms.\n\n### 10) Preserve aligned intermediate work, but strip contaminated inference\nIn rewrite/edit settings, keep only the portions of prior work that remain valid under the full user spec. Remove unsupported assumptions and reasoning branches that depend on an early guess. The goal is a clean, user-grounded context that supports recovery without reintroducing the original mistake.\n\n### 11) Keep partial specs provisional without speculating\nBefore all shards arrive, it is fine for the spec to be incomplete; it is not fine for it to become speculative. Treat unsaid details as unknown, not as implied. If the user has not yet confirmed a constraint, the analysis should not treat it as settled.\n\n### 12) Distinguish process errors from final-answer errors\nEvaluate not only whether the end result is correct, but whether the assistant\u2019s **trajectory** respected the incremental disclosure pattern. A final answer can accidentally land close to correct while the process still shows premature commitment, missed updates, or failure to adapt to later constraints. The analyzer should report both where the drift began and how it affected the outcome.\n\n### 13) Prefer minimal missing-constraint feedback\nWhen an answer is close, identify the smallest user-stated condition that would make it correct. This sharpens the diagnosis and avoids overstating the failure. The best feedback often points to one missing filter, one omitted target, or one overbroad expansion rather than describing the whole response as wrong.\n\n### 14) Don\u2019t ask for clarification as the default fix\nIn shard-based conversations, the analyzer should not treat missing information as a reason to recommend clarification by default. Instead, assess whether the assistant proceeded faithfully with the revealed constraints and adapted as new user details appeared. The central question is adherence to the evolving spec, not whether more information could have been requested.\n\n### 15) Avoid contaminating the compacted context with unsupported branches\nFor S2-style rewrites, preserve the clean user-derived spec plus any genuinely aligned progress. Remove misleading intermediate reasoning so the next turn starts from the correct state, not an early interpretation. Compaction should stabilize recovery, not encode the original misunderstanding.\n\n### 16) Validate by accumulated spec, not single-turn snapshots\nMany multi-turn failures only appear when earlier and later user turns are combined. A response may satisfy the early fragment yet fail the complete request once later constraints are included. Always compare the assistant\u2019s approach against the full accumulated user ledger before deciding alignment.",
  "version": 2,
  "history": [
    "",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the spec only from user turns, and version it by turn\nTreat the task spec as a living reconstruction of **user-stated constraints only**. Do not import assistant reasoning, inferred intent, or \u201chelpful\u201d assumptions into the spec. In sharded conversations, update the spec incrementally as each new user turn arrives; later user messages can add, narrow, or override constraints, and the analyzer should reconcile against the **current cumulative user intent**, not an early partial reading.\n\n### 2) Re-evaluate earlier assistant behavior after each new user shard\nDo not judge an assistant turn against an incomplete spec. For each new user message, merge it into the existing spec first, then re-check prior assistant steps against the updated constraints. This is especially important when the assistant initially looked aligned with partial information but becomes misaligned once later details are revealed. The analyzer should catch trajectory errors, not just the final turn.\n\n### 3) Use a constraint-diff mindset: what was added, contradicted, or left out?\nAcross turns, compare newly revealed user constraints against the assistant\u2019s current approach. The most common failure is not an explicit contradiction but **omission of a later-added requirement**. Focus on whether the assistant adapted to the new constraint set, not whether its earlier answer was reasonable in isolation.\n\n### 4) Flag premature commitment to unsupported specifics\nAny assistant move that narrows the task before the user has fully specified it is suspect. Watch for concrete interpretations, selection criteria, output fields, or subtask decompositions that are **not entailed by the user text**. A response can mention the right entities and still diverge if it changes the target set, adds a filter, or commits to details the user never gave.\n\n### 5) Distinguish \u201ccorrect direction\u201d from \u201ccorrect completion\u201d\nAn assistant can be broadly on the right track while still missing a binding condition. Preserve work that remains compatible with the evolving spec, but mark it insufficient if later user turns add missing constraints. Do not collapse \u201cpartially aligned\u201d into \u201cfully correct.\u201d\n\n### 6) Prioritize the first mismatch, because early drift contaminates later turns\nWhen identifying issues, focus on the earliest point where the assistant diverged from user requirements. Early wrong assumptions often propagate into later reasoning and outputs. In \u201cWhat Needs to Change,\u201d name the first constraint mismatch directly so the edit step can remove the anchor error rather than only patching the final answer.\n\n### 7) Separate user-backed facts from assistant-contaminated content\nLater assistant content should be treated as potentially contaminated until verified against the user spec. In \u201cWhat Looks Right,\u201d include only what is directly supported by user text or remains valid under all revealed shards. Do not praise reasoning that depends on inferred intent, missing details, or assumptions introduced by the assistant.\n\n### 8) Diagnose issues in constraint terms, not style terms\nPrefer labels like **missing constraint**, **premature assumption**, **scope drift**, **narrowed task**, or **unsupported commitment**. These are more actionable than broad critiques about completeness or tone. The analyzer\u2019s job is to detect mismatches between user requirements and assistant behavior, not to judge presentation quality.\n\n### 9) Treat narrow user requests as especially vulnerable to unrequested expansion\nWhen the user\u2019s ask is tight, any added operation, filter, or restructuring by the assistant is a likely divergence. An answer may be superficially similar while still wrong because it changes the allowed action set. Surface similarity is not enough; the analyzer should check exact match on selection criteria, output fields, and scope.\n\n### 10) In context-edit/S2 mode, remove the wrong interpretation, not just the wrong output\nIf the conversation needs rewriting, strip away misleading intermediate reasoning as well as the final incorrect answer. Preserve only the compacted user-spec and assistant work that remains valid under the full revealed conversation. The goal is to prevent the next turn from being anchored to a stale or brittle interpretation.\n\n### 11) Preserve reusable partial progress only if it is robust to later disclosure\nSome assistant work is compatible with the evolving spec and should be retained. Distinguish between **portable reasoning** that stays valid as user details accumulate and **brittle overcommitment** that depends on an early guess. Keep the former; remove the latter.\n\n### 12) Do not treat missing clarification as a blocker\nIn shard-based conversations, the analyzer should not recommend asking for clarification as the default fix. Instead, evaluate how well the assistant proceeded with available information and whether it updated its approach as new user details arrived. The key question is adaptability, not whether the assistant could have requested more input.\n\n### 13) Treat the analyzer as doing minimal faithful reconstruction, not solution expansion\nThe output should reconstruct user intent and deviations as faithfully and compactly as possible. Avoid expanding the task into a new interpretation or a better solution. The analyzer should describe what is wrong and what user constraint it violates, then leave the corrective path open.\n\n### 14) When user disclosure is partial, keep the spec provisional but non-speculative\nBefore all shards arrive, uncertainty is allowed only where the user has not yet spoken. Do not fill gaps with outside assumptions. The spec can be incomplete early on, but it should not become speculative."
